Ladybird grants an unpacked extension its supported API permissions at
first install and saves that grant set. A reload or restore intersects
the manifest with the saved set, so an added declaration isn’t granted
silently. Descriptors carry the sorted grants and the parsed host
patterns, with has_permission() for API code.

Chromium’s PermissionsUpdater::GrantActivePermissions() stores
the grants through ExtensionPrefs::AddGrantedPermissions(),
and InitializePermissions() derives the effective set from
them. Gecko keeps declarations separate from saved user permissions
too, migrating saved grants in ExtensionData.migratePermissions().
And WebKit keeps m_grantedPermissions, set through WebExtensionContext::
setGrantedPermissions(); its hasPermission() ignores a requested
permission that hasn’t been granted. Ladybird matches that
split between declared and granted permissions. Host patterns still come
straight from the manifest.
